# Security headers for statically-served SPA assets (SEC-P7-01).
#
# Cloudflare Workers Static Assets serves this file the same way Cloudflare
# Pages does: a `_headers` file at the root of the static-assets output
# directory (`apps/web/dist/`, since Vite copies `public/` verbatim) is
# parsed at deploy time and applied to matching response paths, independent
# of any Worker code — this is the only way to attach headers to assets
# served directly by the platform without invoking the Worker (`run_worker_
# first` in wrangler.jsonc routes only `/api/*` and `/robots.txt` through the
# Worker; every other path, including `/` and hashed asset files, is served by
# the platform's static-assets layer, which never runs
# `apps/worker/src/index.ts`'s `secureHeaders()` middleware. `/robots.txt` is
# the exception because it must differ per environment — it gets the Worker's
# own, stricter headers rather than this file's). Format: one or more path patterns, each
# followed by indented `Header: value` lines; `/*` matches every path.
# Per-rule limits documented by Cloudflare (shared Pages/Static-Assets
# mechanism): ≤100 header rules per project, ≤2,000 characters per header
# line, ≤100 headers total per `_headers` file.
#
# VERIFICATION NOTE (2026-08-15): the `verify-current-docs` skill's live
# Cloudflare-docs fetch was attempted for this session but the outbound
# proxy returned a blocked/403 response (same restriction noted in prior
# phases for ted.europa.eu/ecb.europa.eu — developers.cloudflare.com was
# likewise unreachable from this dev environment). This `_headers` syntax
# and the Workers-Static-Assets-shares-Pages'-_headers-mechanism claim are
# NOT re-verified against a live fetch this session; they match this
# project's existing recorded understanding (docs/dependency-versions.md's
# "Workers Static Assets is the recommended SPA hosting" note, same platform
# family as Pages) and standard, long-published Cloudflare behavior, but a
# live docs re-check is flagged as a TODO before this ships to production
# (see IMPLEMENTATION_LEDGER.md Phase 7 review-fix entry).
#
# CSP: SPA-compatible mirror of the Worker's own `secureHeaders()` policy
# (apps/worker/src/index.ts) — checked against a real `pnpm build` output
# (apps/web/dist/index.html): no inline <script>/<style>, only a single
# `<script type="module" crossorigin src="/assets/...">` and one external
# `<link rel="stylesheet" crossorigin href="/assets/...">`, both same-origin,
# so no inline-script/inline-style CSP exception (hash, nonce, or the
# unsafe- keyword) is needed. Re-checked 2026-08-22 after route-level code
# splitting landed: the multi-chunk build adds only same-origin
# `<link rel="modulepreload" crossorigin href="/assets/...">` tags, still with
# no inline script, so the policy is unchanged. (Worth re-checking whenever
# the bundler's HTML output changes — the header test reads this file, never
# index.html, so it would not catch a regression here.)
# STAGING ONLY, not in this file: the staging deploy workflow injects
# `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow` into the `/*` block below before
# uploading. It is not committed here because these same assets deploy to
# production, where the marketing pages must stay indexable. See
# .github/workflows/deploy-staging.yml ("Mark staging assets noindex") for
# why the Worker's robots.txt is not sufficient on its own.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://cdn.paddle.com; font-src 'self'; style-src 'self' https://cdn.paddle.com https://sandbox-cdn.paddle.com 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://*.paddle.com; connect-src 'self' https://*.paddle.com; frame-src https://buy.paddle.com https://sandbox-buy.paddle.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), geolocation=(), microphone=()
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
